The boot pool hangs beside its block rather than below it,
so BOOT_CAP_POOL_RAM makes a frame or a pool over the root task's objects,
and revoking below it, as a successor does to end the root task, destroys nothing.
Memory that may hold kernel objects overlaps another node only where one lies below the other.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: memory that may hold kernel objects overlaps a node not above or below it

diff --git a/kernel/boot.c b/kernel/boot.c
--- a/kernel/boot.c
+++ b/kernel/boot.c
@@ -70,7 +70,7 @@ struct thread *boot_create_root(void)
         cap_store(table, BOOT_CAP_POOL_RAM, &pool_ram, root_ram) != KERR_OK) {
         kpanic("cannot fill the root task's table");
     }
-    cap_attach(&table->slots[BOOT_CAP_POOL_RAM], &pool->node);
+    cap_attach(root_ram, &pool->node);
 
